# The oracle image, pinned.
#
# One line per reference, tab-separated: <name> <image-ref> <version> <what it
# answers>. Read by tools/oracle/oracle_env.py, which splits on tabs and takes
# three fields. It is machine-read, not documentation.
#
# All twelve names share one image, which is the shape this library needs and
# not the shape `unicode` and `font` have: a single `make test` reaches for
# several of these references, sometimes in one test binary, so twelve images
# would be twelve things to keep in step for no gain. (This paragraph said
# "five" until libtiff made it twelve; the count had been stale since the
# fourth reference arrived, which is what a number written in prose does.) The problem an image here solves is
# *absence*, not drift. Every reference below is present on the machine this
# was written on, which is why twenty-one tests that reach for one have never
# yet failed to find it, and why the skip they would print reads as success.
#
# Two kinds of pin and they are not the same guarantee. This image is **built here**, so there is no digest to trust: what is
# pinned is everything its Containerfile reads, and the run-time version check
# is the real guarantee. `oracle_env.check_pin()` therefore runs in **both**
# modes, as in `font` and unlike `unicode`, because no line below names a stock
# image whose digest could stand in for the check.
#
# Raising a pin is deliberate. A newer Pillow is newer answers, and a new
# answer that disagrees is a finding to triage rather than a regression to fix.
#
# Building it:
#   podman build -t ghoti-image-oracle-refs:deb13-5 \
#       -f tools/oracle/containers/image-refs/Containerfile tools/oracle
# `make oracle-build` does exactly that. The tag says deb13 because the base is
# what decides four of the five versions, and -1 is a revision that moves when
# the contents do; `:latest` is never a tag here.

# The three decoders that come from Debian at a pinned version.
#
# libjpeg-turbo is reached two ways - through Pillow and through
# tests/tools/jpeg-oracle - and inside this image both spellings are the same
# 2.1.5, which is a change from the host where Pillow's pip wheel bundles its
# own. jpeg_test_utils.cpp tries Pillow first and the dumper otherwise; that
# fallback can no longer change the answer.
pillow	localhost/ghoti-image-oracle-refs:deb13-5	Pillow 11.1.0	Decoded pixels for JPEG, and the reader every verify_*_output.py checks our output with
libjpeg	localhost/ghoti-image-oracle-refs:deb13-5	libjpeg-turbo 2.1.5	Decoded pixels and DCT coefficients, through tests/tools/jpeg-oracle
giflib	localhost/ghoti-image-oracle-refs:deb13-5	giflib 5.2.2	LZW expansion, interlace order, colour tables and geometry - not compositing

# The reference implementation of TIFF, and the one every other TIFF reader
# was written against.
#
# It is not a second reading of the *specification*, and for TIFF that
# distinction has teeth. TIFFReadRGBAImage applies libtiff's own policies
# where the document is silent or where files in the wild disagree with it.
#
# It earned its keep on the first run, which is the argument for wiring an
# oracle up early rather than when a codec is finished. Three differences,
# all of them now measured and none of them guessed at:
#
#   ColorMap    A map storing 8-bit values in a 16-bit field. This codec read
#               it as the specification says and got 1 where libtiff got 255 -
#               a picture nearly black. **Changed to match libtiff**, because
#               the guess can only be wrong for an image whose brightest
#               colour is 0.39% of full scale. The format page had recorded
#               this as an untested warning and had it backwards.
#   alpha       libtiff's RGBA raster is *associated* - its header names the
#               `UaToAa` table that converts to it - and this library's RGBA8
#               is unassociated. Different units, neither wrong; the
#               comparison premultiplies ours, which is one conversion applied
#               to every pixel rather than a tolerance sized to pass a case.
#   photometric libtiff supplies a default for a field section 8 gives none.
#               Deliberately not matched: a file that has not said what its
#               samples mean should be told so.
#
# Twelve of fifteen fixtures are read by both and agree on every sample. The
# other three are the compressed one, which libtiff reads and this codec does
# not, and the two above that only one side reads.
#
# Two traps, both in tests/tools/tiff-oracle/dump_tiff_pixels_libtiff.c where
# they are handled: the RGBA reader returns the raster bottom-up unless asked
# otherwise, which reads exactly like a row-order defect in the codec under
# test; and its samples are ABGR packed into a host-order uint32, which is not
# a byte layout the format defines.
libtiff	localhost/ghoti-image-oracle-refs:deb13-5	libtiff 4.7.0	Decoded RGBA for every TIFF, through tests/tools/tiff-oracle

# The other two BMP decoders tests/data/bmp/bmpsuite_sweep.py corroborates
