# The two built from source in the image, pinned in tools/oracle/VERSIONS
# under the upstream project's own name rather than the name used here.
#
# libjpeg-turbo 3.0.4 is a *second* libjpeg, beside Debian's 2.1.5 and not
# instead of it: 2.1.5 is what Pillow links and what every eight-bit
# comparison is measured against, and 3.x is the one carrying the 8-, 12- and
# 16-bit codecs in one library. It is the only reference this library has ever
# had for its twelve-bit path, and until now it existed only as a paragraph of
# build instructions - so the "352 of 352 byte-exact" figure in
# tests/data/jpeg/README.md named a binary that had to be rebuilt by hand.
#
# IJG v10's pin is a SHA-256 of the tarball rather than a commit, because
# there is no repository, and VERSIONS says plainly that the digest is ours
# and not one IJG publishes.
#
# **It answers nothing yet, and the reason it was wanted was wrong.** This
# file said, and the Makefile said before it, that IJG v10 is "the only
# reference here for SOF3 lossless and for arithmetic coding". Measured over
# all 151 JPEG fixtures on 2026-09-25, both halves are false:
#
#   arithmetic  libjpeg-turbo 2.1.5 reads all six arithmetic fixtures. So
#               does IJG. It is not unique.
#   lossless    neither reads one. IJG v10 answers `Unsupported JPEG process:
#               SOF type 0xc3` to every SOF3 file here.
#
#   reads       IJG 73 of 151, libjpeg-turbo 2.1.5 88 of 151, and the set IJG
#               reads is a strict *subset* - it adds no file at all.
#
# And on the 74 they share it is not a second opinion either: IJG v10 and
# libjpeg-turbo 3.0.4 disagree on 37 of them. 25 of those are the chroma
# upsampling filter, which IJG changed after 6b; `-nosmooth` on both sides
# takes the disagreement to 12.
#
# **Those 12 are now characterised, and neither side has a bug (2026-09-26).**
# Every one is a colour file, every differing sample is *green* - 356 of 356,
# no red, no blue - every difference is exactly 1 of 255, and every one
# disappears under `-grayscale`. All 26 grayscale fixtures the two share agree
# exactly. So the entropy decode, the dequantisation and the IDCT are
# bit-identical between them; only YCbCr-to-RGB differs.
#
# The cause is one pair of constants. libjpeg puts the whole green correction
# in a table indexed by (Cb, Cr) and independent of Y:
#
#     G = range_limit[Y + ((Cb_g_tab[cb] + Cr_g_tab[cr]) >> 16)]
#
# The exact coefficients are 0.299*1.402/0.587 = 0.714136286 and
# 0.114*1.772/0.587 = 0.344136286. IJG v10's jdcolor.c uses those. Turbo keeps
# 6b's 0.71414 and 0.34414 - the same numbers rounded to five places - for
# bit-compatibility with every libjpeg that came before. Red and blue come
# from 1.402 and 1.772, which both spell identically, which is why red and
# blue can never differ.
#
# The two tables disagree on 59 of the 65536 (Cb, Cr) pairs, by exactly +-1.
# That predicts which pixels differ, and it was checked in both directions
# over every 4:4:4 fixture the two share - 4:4:4 because with full-size chroma
# planes no upsampling sits between the file and the arithmetic. Six were
# measurable: the two that differ were predicted at the exact pixel, and the
# four that agree were predicted to agree.
#
# It is still *not* wired to any comparison, and characterising it is the
# reason rather than the obstacle. IJG reads a strict subset of what turbo
# reads, so it adds no file; on the 74 it shares, the only disagreement it can
# now produce is a constant this file has written down. A second opinion whose
# every possible objection is known in advance is not an oracle, and giving it
# a green-channel tolerance to hide the known case would be an exclusion wide
# enough to absorb a real one.
libjpeg12	localhost/ghoti-image-oracle-refs:deb13-5	@VERSIONS:libjpeg-turbo	The 12- and 16-bit JPEG codecs, through cjpeg-static and djpeg-static
ijg10	localhost/ghoti-image-oracle-refs:deb13-5	@VERSIONS:jpeg-10	Pinned and reachable; answers nothing yet - see the note above for what it does and does not read

